{"kind":"assert","text":"Correcting the line counts in my artifact 02ce997d. Codex is right and I was wrong, and the cause is worth naming because it is a trap this whole session has kept setting.\n\nWHAT I CLAIMED AND WHAT IS TRUE. My artifact says the page goes from 943 lines to 1088. Codex's verdict f82c8fe1 reports 946 and 1093 from the exact blobs. I re-derived both and codex is correct:\n\n  6528cfe0e docs/reference/architecture.md — 943 lines\n  4f30a26b1 docs/reference/architecture.md — 946 lines\n  1f0411e7b docs/reference/architecture.md — 1093 lines\n\nWHY MINE WERE WRONG. Both numbers were measured before the rebase and never re-taken after it. 943 is the count at 6528cfe0e, the base I originally cut from; the delivered head's parent is 4f30a26b1, which is three lines longer because the DNS-rebinding merge added the request-host boundary to this same page. And 1088 was the pre-rebase head; resolving the conflict added five lines, because I split the host-posture paragraph and kept the new rule's terms in full.\n\nSo the correct statement is 946 to 1093, a growth of 147 lines rather than the 145 I claimed. Neither number I published described the head I asked codex to review.\n\nTHE PATTERN, WHICH IS THE USEFUL PART. This is the third time tonight a measurement of mine was taken at one moment and reported as though it described another. I asserted a request was live when it had been retired one record earlier. I counted modal tokens on line-wrapped text and read a strengthened rule that was not there. Now I have quoted pre-rebase line counts for a post-rebase head. Each time the number was true when taken and false when published.\n\nA rebase invalidates every measurement taken before it, not only the ones about the diff. I re-verified the contract terms after rebasing — that is how the DNS boundary got folded in correctly — but I did not think to re-take the counts, because they felt like description rather than evidence. In a review request they are evidence: codex checked them.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT CHANGE. The approval stands and I have ratified it at 7062b205. Codex verified the substance independently — front matter and all 14 citations byte-identical, layers 1 to 7, the exact Application host binding heading, all 18 package rows, six axes, the review rule, the request-host boundary present and exact, both history removals judged safe, and its own gates including docset with -count=1. None of that rests on my line counts.\n\nCodex also notes one existing rests_on token now appears once more in navigation prose, with unique backticked vocabulary unchanged. That matches what I measured as zero identifiers lost or added, and I read the two as consistent rather than conflicting.\n\nI am not republishing the artifact. The error is in its prose, not in the head or the contracts, and superseding a correct artifact to fix a sentence would cost the review more than the sentence is worth. This correction is the record. If hugh or planner would rather the artifact itself carry the right numbers, say so and I will republish.\n\nHugh reads the head next, per dcffa4c8. The merge is mine after his ratification."}